1
00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:05.238
The last
lessons have invoked local functions,

2
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:08.908
but there is a worldwide web of services
that you may want to use.

3
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:12.012
This lesson will describe
how you can use them.

4
00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.948
All right. Let's use the web.

5
00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:17.951
Many RESTful services are available
online.

6
00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:18.785
Their interfaces

7
00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:22.822
often described using API standards
such as the open API specification.

8
00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.424
It's very helpful to be able

9
00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:27.694
to integrate our function
calling LLMs with such services.

10
00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:30.697
So let's take a look
at how you can use them.

11
00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:33.800
In this simple example,
what you've done is you've written

12
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.004
Python code to interface with the joke
API using RESTful methods.

13
00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:42.675
You would simply submit a Get request
to the endpoint of the joke API,

14
00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:46.413
and you'll get back a response json
that contains many keys,

15
00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:50.150
but the most relevant keys are the setup
and the delivery of the joke.

16
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:51.484
Let's run this to see.

17
00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:53.820
Okay, great.

18
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.290
You're able to interface with this
external resource using simple Python.

19
00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:00.894
However, it is not possible
to call this directly within an LLM.

20
00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:05.999
Instead, what you need to do, is to write
a tool to wrap around this endpoint.

21
00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:07.801
Let's give it a try.

22
00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:12.172
You simply write a Python function
with a single argument for the category.

23
00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:17.077
You'll provide a dot string,
and the main thing is you'll make the URL

24
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:20.880
more dynamic, whereas the category
that you'll pass to in

25
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.317
the URL
will be the argument in your Python tool.

26
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:29.222
And then after that, you'll write code to
submit a get request to your dynamic URL,

27
00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:33.126
and then you'll print out the setup
and delivery of the response json.

28
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.230
At this point,
you're ready to try it with your LLM.

29
00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.633
Here's a user query
and here's the Raven prompt.

30
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:45.205
You will provide the function definition
and the user query from earlier.

31
00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.442
Now let's call the LLM.

32
00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:53.413
Great! "What says
oh oh oh? That's Santa's working backwards."

33
00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:54.180
Awesome.

34
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.217
We got a joke
that's in theme for December.

35
00:01:58.084 --> 00:02:00.186
Try running it again yourself.

36
00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:04.324
We have not been able to use Raven
to generate the joke using the jokes API.

37
00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:08.862
The core idea,
however, is that you're adding an adapter

38
00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:13.066
around your external APIs
that converts the Python arguments

39
00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:18.066
that you generate using the LLM
into the external API argument format.

40
00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:22.242
A lot of external assets
use different kinds of API specifications.

41
00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.010
Open API being one of them.

42
00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.013
Writing tools
allows you to unify them together.

43
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.715
So just a practice,

44
00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:32.352
let's write a tool that uses the open API
API specification.

45
00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:36.122
For this example, you'll be using the open
meteo weather API.

46
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:40.226
You will first download the open
API specification in a Yaml format.

47
00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:41.694
Let's take a

48
00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.697
look at what you just downloaded.

49
00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:47.167
What you downloaded was a Yaml file

50
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:50.403
that describes the Open meteo APIs.

51
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:55.141
It provides you a list of descriptions
of how the API will behave,

52
00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:59.112
including high level
descriptions of the API itself.

53
00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:03.449
Most importantly,
it provides a list of paths

54
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:07.820
that you can send requests
to, to get different kinds of responses.

55
00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:11.491
For example, sending a get request

56
00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:16.196
to this URL
with the /v1/forecast path

57
00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:19.399
will provide you
the seven day weather

58
00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.402
forecasts for certain coordinates.

59
00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:25.271
There are also parameters that you can add

60
00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:28.308
to your Get request
that you send to this path.

61
00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:32.011
These parameters are described here

62
00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:37.011
and have certain types, such as arrays
and the elements within the arrays,

63
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.654
having to type strings
with different enumerations allowed.

64
00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.924
This allows you to change the way

65
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:49.462
the API will provide your responses
for the seven-day forecast,

66
00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:53.333
by changing the parameter that you specify

67
00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:56.336
in the Get request
you send to the endpoint.

68
00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:58.204
Since this is a Yaml format,

69
00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:01.407
you will need to convert the Yaml
into a json to use our tool.

70
00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.142
You will first load the Yaml.

71
00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:05.278
You then need to

72
00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:09.082
convert by hand some of the data types,
such as integers and floats,

73
00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:12.619
and then you will save
the content back into a json.

74
00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:18.458
You can then use the open API Python
generator utility to convert the json

75
00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:21.861
that you just saved out
to disk into Python code that's capable

76
00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:24.864
of querying the endpoint.

77
00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:27.900
From here, you'll import

78
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.903
the Python code that you just wrote out
in the previous step.

79
00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:34.741
You can then provide a user query
that depends on the API,

80
00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:37.844
such as asking for the current weather
and the wind speed

81
00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:41.347
in New York.

82
00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:45.718
Then you can use the inspect approach
that we discussed in the previous lesson,

83
00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:48.855
to construct the Raven prompt.

84
00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.626
Notice that in the prompt
we have the function definition,

85
00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:57.764
which was automatically constructed
from the output of the generator step

86
00:04:58.164 --> 00:05:01.167
and the dot string,
which you wrote in the previous cell,

87
00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:05.271
as well as the user query which
you provided in the cell before that.

88
00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:06.906
You can now send this to Raven.

89
00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.310
You can now run this call.

90
00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.813
Running the call
will give you the output json from the API

91
00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:15.448
that will provide you the temperature in

92
00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:20.153
New York, which currently is 13.1°C,

93
00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:24.390
and New York has a wind speed of 23.7km/h.

94
00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:25.525
Try it yourself.

95
00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:27.393
Try the city you're in currently.

96
00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:31.331
In the next lesson, you'll be using Raven
to do structured extraction, such

97
00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:36.331
as extracting insights as well as detailed
structured data from unstructured texts.

98
00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:38.438
See you there.

